From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: touch on a touch display: taps, scrolling, drags, right clicks, pinch

What: one gesture recognizer on the main view and on every other display's
view takes every direct touch (a touch display's, a Sidecar iPad's; never a
mouse or a trackpad, those keep the views' own methods) and begins at
touch-down. It hands the rules (ui/omacvm-touch.h) the fingers' centre and
their own movement (the mean over the fingers in both frames), with the
events' times, and the guest gets what they decide: the pointer to the
finger (omacvmQueuePlace: the mapping omacvmQueuePointer used, now after
its guard), buttons, scrolling on the precise-scroll path (its own
remainder; OMACVM_TOUCH_SCROLL wheel units per point, 1 by default), and a
coast on a timer in the common run loop modes (AppKit tracks touches in
the event-tracking mode) that the next touch stops. A touch with another
app in front brings OmacVM forward and takes the pointer, as a click does.
While exactly two fingers touch the rules also get both, the same one first
every time; a pinch's two fingers go to the guest's touch screen
(virtio-multitouch, which the app attaches on a Mac with a touch display;
omacvm-virtio-multitouch-touch-only.patch) as contacts 0 and 1, each at its
share of the output touched (omacvmShare: the mapping's first half, shared
with omacvmQueuePlace). The guest's compositor puts a touch screen on its
focused output: the rules send the pointer between the fingers when the
second lands, which makes that the output touched. Without the device a
pinch sends nothing. The made-up contacts of a trackpad's magnification
(qemu-cocoa-pinch-zoom.patch, for its virtio-pinch touchpad, which OmacVM
does not attach) are not sent when the guest has a touch screen: they would
land on it as two fingers on the screen's middle row.
Off unless the app sets OMACVM_TOUCH=1 (its Touch display switch,
experimental, off by default, and a touch display): no recognizer, AppKit's
emulation as before. qemu.log at start: "cocoa: touch: taps, scrolling,
hold-drag and right click on a touch display", the scrolling's gain and
"a two-finger pinch goes to the guest's touch screen (virtio-multitouch)"
or "does nothing (the guest has no touch screen: no virtio-multitouch)", or
"AppKit's mouse emulation (OMACVM_TOUCH is not 1)".

Why: AppKit gives a view that overrides the mouse methods its generic mouse
emulation, made inside the app (measured on the touch display, #843): a tap
is entered, moved, down and up 0.2 ms apart at the lift, then exited; a
touch that moves is mouseDown at once, then a left drag (no scroll events
ever: a scroll selected text, a tap that slid tore a browser tab off); one
held about 730 ms is the right button. The real cursor never moves: the
emulated mouseMoved carries the jump from it to the finger, so in full
screen the corner guard moved the guest's pointer by that delta from its
own place and taps landed off by up to the zone's 200 points. A
recognizer that begins at touch-down takes the touch from the emulation
(none of its events came); AppKit's own press recognizer waited for the
click recognizer to fail and lost to the emulation's right button, hence
one recognizer deciding everything. Direct touches reach an app only
through recognizers (not event taps or monitors: TN3212), so this is the
only place for it.

Why a touch screen for the pinch (#843): Linux has two ways to a pinch. A
touchpad's goes through libinput's gesture recognition to the app under the
pointer; a touch screen's contacts go to the app under the fingers, which
recognizes the pinch itself and zooms between them, as macOS does on its
touch display. Chromium, Firefox and GTK 4 do (Chromium 153 on Hyprland
0.56.2, measured with a uinput touch screen: 1.0 to 3.9 and back, two
fingers moving together nothing). A touch display's touches reach only the
app touched, never OmacVM's Gestures helper, so its way (the fingers to the
guest's virtual trackpad) is not open here. One-finger touches stay on the
tablet and the wheel: apps without touch support keep working, and the
guest sees a touch only while two fingers pinch.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's display windows); the idea would suit
QEMU's cocoa UI too.
Test: app/runtime/Tests/touch/test-touch.sh (the rules; with the patched
ui/cocoa.m the wiring; --self-test: every wiring check fails without the
patch).

